    "html": "<p><strong>This is an exam checkpoint.</strong> Canvas labels Week 12 as the Midterm Period for Modules 3 and 4; it has no separate lesson self-checks. Focus on connections among literary periods, genres, and social questions.</p><h2>Five-minute review route</h2><ol><li>Explain Renaissance humanism, Enlightenment reason, Romantic imagination, Victorian realism/Gothic, Modernist fragmentation, and postmodern self-awareness.</li><li>Retell Hamlet, Candide, Don Quixote, Jane Eyre, Mrs Dalloway, Ulysses, and “Tickets, Please!” in one sentence each.</li><li>Identify the author or tradition behind each key title; say “anonymous” when that is more accurate.</li><li>Distinguish point of view from an author’s belief, and fact from interpretation.</li><li>Explain how a work critiques social power, imperialism, or industrial conditions using one story detail.</li><li>Define stream of consciousness, sublime, verisimilitude, Bildungsroman, metafiction, and alienation.</li></ol><div class=\"note\"><strong>Exam habit:</strong> Learn the story before the interpretation. A reliable answer often starts with who wants what, what blocks them, what changes, and how the ending qualifies the theme.</div>\n\n**Checkpoint practice:** Choose any works in this week’s review route and retell each as author → character → conflict → ending. These are supplemental prompts, not Canvas self-checks."
